                <pb facs="0421" n="349" sameAs="frus:frus1909/pg349"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 18942/8–9.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to Minister
                    Heimké.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1909-05-13"><hi rendition="#italic">May 13,
                        1909</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 74.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    No. 185 of the 19th ultimo, reporting the steps you have taken to have
                    investigation made of the lynching of William Wright, a colored American
                    citizen, by a mob of Guatemalans last December; and that after you receive the
                    report of the investigation you will take the matter up with the Guatemalan
                    Government.</p>
                <p>Your course in the case is approved by the department, which awaits the results
                    of the investigation.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Huntington
                    Wilson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
